Serve writable files, and survive a call we cannot serve

The sensor registry is not a read-only blob: before the DSP can bring up the
registry it writes a scratch file next to it,

    /mnt/vendor/persist/sensors/registry/registry/../temp.json

and sns_reg.conf points its 'output=' at the registry directory too. hexagonrpcd
0.4.0 answers every write-mode fopen() with AEE_EUNSUPPORTED, so registry init
fails, the sensor PD dies and takes the ADSP -- and the sound card -- with it.

Adds apps_std_fwrite (method 5) and the hexagonfs write path behind it.

Two departures from the version this is based on
(Sr-0w/hotdog-linux-bringup, aports/main/hexagonrpcd):

  - The open mode is carried through as open(2) flags rather than a bare
    'create' bool. fopen("w") must truncate; without O_TRUNC a second, shorter
    write leaves the tail of the first one behind, and a registry file that is
    valid up to byte N and garbage after it is worse than one that never got
    written.

  - The listener clears `sc` before continuing past a failed call. `sc` still
    describes the call that failed, and outbufs_calculate_size() walks
    REMOTE_SCALARS_OUTBUFS(sc) entries of a `returned` array that is NULL here,
    so any failed call declaring an output buffer would segfault the daemon.

The Oppo-specific /proc/oppoVersion and /sys/project_info mappings from that
same patch are left out, and so is its rpcd_builder.c hunk, which is already
covered by 0001.

Upstream issues: linux-msm/hexagonrpc#19, linux-msm/hexagonrpc#21

